# NoiseModelling Benchmark

Compares several NoiseModelling versions on the same datasets (Clisson and La Montagne, France) and publishes the results as a
static website on GitHub Pages: <https://universite-gustave-eiffel.github.io/NoiseModellingBenchmark/>

Development currently happens on the fork
<https://github.com/pierromond/NoiseModellingBenchmark>; the PR workflow and the
`pr-data` branch use the repository that runs the workflow.

## Where is what

| Path | What it does |
|---|---|
| `benchmark/versions.json` | the list of versions to compare: `version -> release zip URL`. An **empty URL** marks the "head" version, taken from the NoiseModelling CI binary |
| `benchmark/config/benchmark.json` | **single source of truth**: datasets, Java mapping, silence threshold, histogram bins, per-version simulation parameters and the V6 reference models |
| `benchmark/drivers/benchmark_run_clisson.sh` | Clisson entry point — thin wrapper around `benchmark/scripts/bench.sh --dataset clisson` |
| `benchmark/drivers/benchmark_run_montagne.sh` | La Montagne entry point — thin wrapper around `benchmark/scripts/bench.sh --dataset montagne` |
| `benchmark/scripts/bench.sh` | the actual orchestration (download, run, aggregate), parameterised by `--dataset` |
| `benchmark/scripts/lib/common.sh` | shared bash helpers: versions, download, binary discovery |
| `benchmark/scripts/ci/` | CI helpers (`nm_versions.py`, `github_artifacts.py`) kept out of the workflow YAML |
| `benchmark/python/bench/config.py` | loads `benchmark/config/benchmark.json` |
| `benchmark/python/bench/stats.py` | shared receiver parsing, LAEQ marking and silence threshold |
| `benchmark/python/bench/compare.py` | unified comparison logic for both datasets |
| `benchmark/python/compare_versions.py` | builds the Clisson pairwise comparisons (`comparisons.json`) — thin entry point over `benchmark/python/bench/compare.py` |
| `benchmark/python/compare_versions_montagne.py` | builds the La Montagne comparisons — thin entry point over `benchmark/python/bench/compare.py` |
| `benchmark/scripts/bench_pr.sh` | local/CI entry point for a pull-request benchmark (isolated from official results) |
| `benchmark/scripts/ci/nm_pr.py` | resolves a NoiseModelling PR to its CI artifact and benchmark label |
| `benchmark/python/compare_pr.py` | compares a PR result with the published versions and writes `website/data/pr/<pr>/` |
| `benchmark/python/build_baseline_pack.py` | builds the compact baseline artifact used by the PR workflow |
| `.github/workflows/benchmark-pr.yml` | manual workflow: run, compare, comment and optionally publish a PR benchmark |
| `website/build.py` | builds `website/index.html` from `website/template.html`, `benchmark/config/benchmark.json` and the JSON files |
| `website/template.html` | the page structure (HTML only) |
| `website/js/` | the page JavaScript, split into ordered modules: `00-config`, `01-theme`, `02-helpers`, `03-cards`, `04-header`, `05-map`, `06-comparisons`, `07-histogram`, `08-boxplot`, `09-scatter`, `10-density`, `11-diffmap`, `12-montagne`, `13-montagne-map`, `14-start`, `18-pull-requests`, `15-sidebar`, `16-boot`. `js/00-constants.js` (histogram bins, silence threshold) is generated from `benchmark/config/benchmark.json` |
| `website/index.css` | all the page CSS (dark and light themes in one file) |
| `website/data/` | **generated, not in git**: `results.json`, `comparisons.json`, `settings.json`, `build.json`, `start.json`, and one folder of map layers per version |
| `website/data/pr/` | **generated, non-official**: `index.json`, one `manifest.json` and comparison files per pull request |
| `benchmark/simulations/` | the simulation scripts launched by NoiseModelling: `runscriptV5.0.groovy`, `runscriptV6.0.groovy`, plus one script per version when the API changed |
| `benchmark/simulations/lib/NoiseBench.groovy` | shared simulation helpers: script loading, profiler CSV, LAEQ statistics |
| `benchmark/input/clisson/` | the Clisson raw dataset (`clisson/`) and the benchmark dataset (`cropped/`, Git LFS) |
| `benchmark/input/montagne/` | the La Montagne dataset (Git LFS) and its measured reference `measure/RECEIVERS_LEVEL.geojson` |
| `schemas/` | JSON schemas of the generated artifacts, checked by `tools/validate_outputs.py` |
| `tools/` | the characterization harness: `run_checks.sh`, `snapshot.py`, `check.py`, `sim_spec.py`, `check_groovy.py`, `test_noisebench.py` |
| `docs/contracts.md` | the frozen compatibility contract (what must never change) |
| `.github/workflows/benchmark.yml` | the CI: one job matrix per Java version, the "head" job, the website build, the Pages deploy |
| `LICENSE` | GPL-3.0 |

## How it works

1. `benchmark/drivers/benchmark_run_clisson.sh --version v5.0.1` downloads that release, then NoiseModelling's ScriptRunner runs
   `benchmark/simulations/runscriptV5.0.groovy` on `benchmark/input/clisson/cropped/`; results land in `benchmark/output/v5.0.1/`.
   The script to load and its parameters come from `benchmark/config/benchmark.json`, not from hard-coded branches.
2. `benchmark/drivers/benchmark_run_clisson.sh --aggregate-only` turns `benchmark/output/*/` into `website/data/` and computes the comparisons.
3. `benchmark/drivers/benchmark_run_montagne.sh --version v5.0.1` runs `benchmark/simulations/montagne/montagneV5.groovy` on
   `benchmark/input/montagne/`; results land in `benchmark/output/montagne/v5.0.1/`, and `--aggregate-only` builds
   `website/data/montagne/` (including the comparison to the measured levels).
4. The CI does 1–3 for every version of `benchmark/versions.json`, then publishes `website/` on GitHub Pages.

Both entry points are thin wrappers; the orchestration lives in `benchmark/scripts/bench.sh`.

## Run it locally

```bash
bash benchmark/drivers/benchmark_run_clisson.sh --version v5.0.1   # one Clisson version
bash benchmark/drivers/benchmark_run_clisson.sh                    # every version of benchmark/versions.json (skips the head)
bash benchmark/drivers/benchmark_run_clisson.sh --aggregate-only   # only rebuild the Clisson site data from benchmark/output/
bash benchmark/drivers/benchmark_run_montagne.sh --version v5.0.1  # one La Montagne version
bash benchmark/drivers/benchmark_run_montagne.sh --aggregate-only  # only rebuild the La Montagne site data from benchmark/output/montagne/
```

Needs Python 3, git-lfs (for `benchmark/input/clisson/`) and Java 11 for v4/v5 or Java 25 for v6.
The NoiseModelling binary is downloaded automatically from the release URL in `benchmark/versions.json`.

## Benchmark a pull request

A pull request is never added to `benchmark/versions.json`. It gets a unique label
(`v6.0.2-SNAPSHOT-pr1092-680a381`) and its data is written to
`website/data/pr/<pr>/`, outside the official results.

Locally:

```bash
bash benchmark/scripts/bench_pr.sh --pr 1092
bash benchmark/scripts/bench_pr.sh --pr 1092 --dataset montagne
bash benchmark/scripts/bench_pr.sh --pr 1092 --artifact-zip /tmp/NoiseModelling-1092_merge.zip
```

The script resolves the PR's successful `CI.yml` run, downloads its
`NoiseModelling-*` artifact (using `GH_TOKEN`, `GITHUB_TOKEN` or `gh auth token`),
runs Clisson and/or La Montagne with the normal benchmark configuration, and
compares the result with the published versions. Use `--artifact-zip` when the
artifact was downloaded manually.

From GitHub, use **Actions → Benchmark PR → Run workflow** (or
`gh workflow run benchmark-pr.yml -f pr=1092`). The workflow needs a `GH_PAT`
secret with read access to the NoiseModelling Actions artifacts; without it,
download the artifact manually and use the local `--artifact-zip` path. It:

- reruns the selected baseline once on the same runner (`paired=true`,
  `baseline=v6.0.2-SNAPSHOT` by default) so that compute times are comparable;
  no repeated runs are made. Use `paired=false` to skip the extra run and keep
  the historical baseline timing;
- uploads the JSON report and site data as artifacts;
- posts a summary on the NoiseModelling PR only when `comment=true` (disabled by
  default while testing);
- with `publish=true`, pushes the PR data to the `pr-data` branch, where the
  **Pull requests** tab reads it. PR data is clearly marked as non-official and
  is excluded from the official results.

## Tests and guardrails

A refactor is only safe if the observable outputs stay identical. `tools/run_checks.sh` runs every
guard and must stay green:

| Tool | What it locks |
|---|---|
| `tools/check.py` | the pipeline and site outputs (normalised hashes) against `tools/baseline/snapshot.json` |
| `tools/sim_spec.py` | the simulation contract (script + parameters per version) against `tools/baseline/simulation_spec.json` |
| `tools/check_groovy.py` | every Groovy script still parses |
| `tools/test_noisebench.py` | the shared `lib/NoiseBench.groovy` helpers |
| `tools/check_js.py` | every website JS module parses (`node --check`) |
| `tools/render_check.py` | the rendered pages (Chrome headless) match `tools/baseline/render.json` |
| `tools/validate_outputs.py` | the generated artifacts match `schemas/` |

```bash
bash tools/run_checks.sh          # regenerate outputs and check everything
bash tools/run_checks.sh --no-run # check the current state without regenerating
```

When a change is intentional, accept the new baseline explicitly:

```bash
python3 tools/snapshot.py --update    # outputs
python3 tools/sim_spec.py --update    # simulation contract
```

The list of things that must never change is in `docs/contracts.md`.

## Rules that keep the results comparable

- A version runs with **Java 25** when its name starts with `v6`, with **Java 11** otherwise.
- Levels ≤ **-89 dB** are treated as `NaN`: excluded from means, histograms and comparisons (they appear as a
  `NaN` column in the histogram).
- The unreleased "head" version can be either an entry **without URL** in `benchmark/versions.json` (built from a
  NoiseModelling CI artifact; on a fork this needs the optional `GH_PAT` secret and depends on a non-expired
  artifact being found) or an entry pinned to a **Maven Central snapshot URL** (built deterministically by the
  normal matrix). `v6.0.2-SNAPSHOT` is pinned this way; a new unreleased version is auto-added without URL by the CI.
- The Clisson benchmark uses `benchmark/input/clisson/cropped/`: a 500 m x 500 m receiver area with a 1.25 km buffer of buildings, roads and DEM around it. The grid spacing is unchanged and the recommended propagation parameters are used (`confMaxSrcDist=800 m`, `confMaxReflDist=350 m`).
- Do not modify `benchmark/input/clisson/clisson/` or `benchmark/input/clisson/cropped/`: published results would no longer be comparable.
- For La Montagne, the comparison to the measurements is **calibrated per version**: `offset = measured − computed`
  at the receiver closest to the source (`benchmark/input/montagne/LW_ROADS.geojson`), so the error is zero there for every model.
- Do not modify `benchmark/input/montagne/` nor `benchmark/input/montagne/measure/`: published results would no longer be comparable.
